# Line endings and encoding are repo policy, not a property of whoever's
# machine last touched the file.
#
# Why this file exists: `git config core.autocrlf` is true on the build
# machine, so the working tree legitimately holds CRLF while the committed
# blobs hold LF. That works right up until it doesn't — a BOM reached a
# committed JSON once this way, and `git archive` on this checkout emitted CRLF
# into the tarball a judge would download. Neither of those is visible in a
# normal diff.
#
# text=auto  git decides text vs binary per file by content (the same detection
#            core.autocrlf already used, so declaring it changes nothing about
#            what is currently committed) and normalises text to LF on the way
#            INTO the repo.
# eol=lf     text files come back OUT as LF — on checkout, and in `git archive`.
#            This is the half core.autocrlf was overriding.
#
# Deliberately NOT here: a blanket `*.stl -text`. 15 of the 16 tracked STLs are
# ASCII, one is binary, and pinning the extension either way would misclassify
# the rest. Content detection gets every one of them right; it was measured
# (`git ls-files --eol`) before this file was written.
#
# What this file cannot do is detect. It sets policy going forward; the guard
# that checks the policy held is `npm run leak-audit`, which fails on any
# committed blob with CRLF and on any file that starts with a UTF-8 BOM.
#
# NOTE: this was added WITHOUT `git add --renormalize`. The 230 blobs committed
# before it were already verified LF-only and BOM-free, and rewriting them to
# prove a point they already satisfy would have buried the real history of this
# build under a whole-repo whitespace commit.

* text=auto eol=lf

# The three files content detection already calls binary, pinned so a future
# edit to one of them cannot be re-detected as text and normalised.
*.png binary
